{"case_id":"EU-RRF-Q01","question":"How much new RRF cash did Sweden receive in the payment reported on 14 October 2026? Report in million EUR; for reported numeric amounts use the precision given in the source.","sources":[{"licence":"CC BY 4.0","licence_url":"https://commission.europa.eu/legal-notice_en","published_on":"2026-10-14","sha256_text":"2468e5b2add32ae797aea049b4ba6252b67e981bf38da5bfcbafd6eac21cf9fc","source_id":"EU-RRF-20261014","text":"European Commission - Daily News Daily News 14 / 10 / 2026 Brussels, 14 October 2026 Commission disburses close to €1 billion in RRF payments to Sweden and the Netherlands Today, the European Commission disbursed nearly €1 billion to Sweden and the Netherlands under the Recovery and Resilience Facility (RRF). The Facility is the centrepiece of NextGenerationEU – the Commission's post-pandemic programme to support Member States' recovery, economic growth, and competitiveness. Sweden received €333 million, marking its third and final payment under the RRF. The payment supports reforms and investments linked to the green transition and improvements in labour and housing markets. These include measures to strengthen the development and deployment of innovative industrial technologies with zero, low or negative greenhouse gas emissions; expand enrolment in higher education; and increase the supply of new energy-efficient rental housing. Sweden submitted its final payment request on 2 July 2026, which the Commission greenlighted on 31 August 2026 . Following a favourable opinion from the Council's Economic and Financial Committee and the Commission's payment decision, the funds have now been disbursed. With this final instalment, Sweden has received its full allocation of €3.45 billion, having fulfilled all milestones and targets in its national recovery and resilience plan. The Netherlands received €661 million, marking its fourth RRF payment. The measures linked to this disbursement include reforms such as the transition to a new pension system, as well as investments in offshore wind environmental protection, energy-saving home upgrades, green hydrogen research and demonstration projects, digital education solutions, and up- and reskilling for unemployed people. The Dutch authorities submitted their fourth payment request on 2 July 2026, which the Commission approved on 7 September 2026 . The funds were released after a favourable opinion from the Council's Economic and Financial Committee and the Commission's payment decision. Overall, the Netherlands' plan is financed by €5.44 billion in grants. With today's payment, total disbursements to the country reach 68.6% of its allocation, while 81.1% of the plan's milestones and targets have been completed. As with all Member States, RRF payments are performance-based and depend on the successful delivery of agreed reforms and investments. With the Facility due to close at the end of 2026, Member States had to complete all outstanding milestones and targets by August 2026 and submit final payment requests by the end of September. An interactive map showcasing examples of reforms and investments supported by the RRF is available online. Further details on the RRF payment claim process are also available online.","title":"Commission disburses close to €1 billion in RRF payments to Sweden and the Netherlands (Daily News 14 / 10 / 2026)","url":"https://ec.europa.eu/commission/presscorner/detail/en/mex_26_2159"}],"target_unit":"million EUR"}"}}
